  "articleSummary": "**Summary:**\n\n* **Core Thesis:** The primary AI security threat is not a single sci-fi breakthrough, but the compounding multiplication of agent endurance (task length) and breadth (orchestrated sub-agents), weaponized via \"LLMjacking\" where attackers use stolen credentials to make victims pay for infinite autonomous attack attempts.\n* **Key Technologies & Concepts:** Autonomous AI agents, multi-agent orchestrators (e.g., Claude Code, GTG-1002 cyber-espionage campaign), METR task-horizon curves, and LLMjacking (cloud credential theft routing token bills to victims).\n* **Tone & Framing:** Pragmatic, cautionary analysis framed through *The Terminator* (\"absolutely will not stop... billing\") and economic math, warning that traditional access gates and per-attempt defense models fail against zero-marginal-cost swarms.\n* **Intended Audience:** Software engineering teams, AI developers, and cybersecurity professionals skeptical of agentic threats.",
